WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.678
SpareCycle Review's first customer hypothesis is a refurbishment team that owns its diagnostic software.

2
00:00:05.858 --> 00:00:14.296
A technician observes a problem. An engineer needs a controlled fix and proof. Follow that handoff into the working backend, then the investment roadmap.

3
00:00:14.976 --> 00:00:22.505
A technician records passport evidence. An engineer defines the software issue. A separate reviewer inspects the tested candidate.

4
00:00:22.685 --> 00:00:28.773
The output is a draft pull request for code review. Passing software tests never certify a physical repair.

5
00:00:29.453 --> 00:00:34.611
The company workspace connects a passport, project, and change request to a particular evidence revision.

6
00:00:34.791 --> 00:00:42.234
These records and accounts are synthetic. The provider calls, test executions, and resulting draft pull requests actually happened.

7
00:00:42.914 --> 00:00:48.538
Here, a valid zero-percent battery reading becomes missing data. That can mislead diagnosis.

8
00:00:48.718 --> 00:00:56.141
The request defines expected behavior before generation. The backend compares original and candidate code using exactly the same test cases.

9
00:00:56.821 --> 00:01:04.059
These saved private-run timestamps document Nano investigation, passing verification, Super review, and a draft created after separate approval.

10
00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:10.292
This is recorded execution evidence. The intervals include pauses, so they are not a latency benchmark.

11
00:01:10.972 --> 00:01:18.695
Nebius Token Factory hosts NVIDIA Nemotron inference. GitHub Actions runs isolated tests; Nebius Sandboxes are not the live runner.

12
00:01:18.875 --> 00:01:24.928
Relevant private code goes to Nebius. Test isolation does not mean reasoning stays on the company's laptop.

13
00:01:25.608 --> 00:01:33.027
Lightning investigated the public case; Nano investigated the private pilot. Super proposed and independently reviewed both candidates.

14
00:01:33.207 --> 00:01:40.275
Ultra was separately checked, not proven better. Future routing should follow evaluations of correctness, latency, and cost.

15
00:01:40.955 --> 00:01:46.874
The supplied Nebius usage view records four model tiers. It confirms usage, not correctness.

16
00:01:47.054 --> 00:01:54.572
Rounded cost buckets cannot establish cost per accepted fix. That requires reconciled usage across successful and rejected attempts.

17
00:01:55.252 --> 00:02:03.149
The harness bounds evidence, requires structured output, and validates changes. The model has no shell, network, or publishing tool.

18
00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:10.208
Trusted server code controls testing and publication. The model proposes; the application authorizes each transition.

19
00:02:10.888 --> 00:02:15.911
A reserved stage budget, forty-second deadline, and bounded input and output constrain each call.

20
00:02:16.091 --> 00:02:24.089
Current execution is a pure JavaScript diagnostic-parser change. Broader languages and dependencies need additional test profiles and evaluations.

21
00:02:24.769 --> 00:02:31.457
The candidate is bound to its base commit, patch hash, and evidence revision. Proof and approval refer to those inputs.

22
00:02:31.637 --> 00:02:37.291
Changed code or evidence invalidates old approval. Passing proof cannot authorize a different candidate.

23
00:02:37.971 --> 00:02:44.254
GitHub Actions executes both versions in a network-disabled container. The original has one failure and three passes.

24
00:02:44.434 --> 00:02:51.382
The candidate passes all four unchanged test cases. That proves covered behavior, not every input or hardware condition.

25
00:02:52.062 --> 00:02:58.031
The model reviewer receives fresh context. A different organization member approves the exact tested candidate.

26
00:02:58.211 --> 00:03:05.859
The private pilot rejected self-approval. The QA accounts are synthetic; this demonstrates a permission boundary, not customer adoption.

27
00:03:06.539 --> 00:03:13.262
This actual App-created draft contains the tested patch and verification metadata. Technician narratives remain in the workspace.

28
00:03:13.442 --> 00:03:18.656
A maintainer still reviews the code. Nothing is automatically merged or marked physically repaired.

29
00:03:19.336 --> 00:03:26.699
Encrypted dispatch ran verification in the same private repository. One baseline failure became four unchanged test cases passing.

30
00:03:26.879 --> 00:03:35.726
A separate approval preceded a real draft. This validates one fixture. Private setup currently requires an operator-approved verifier and repository key.

31
00:03:36.406 --> 00:03:43.469
Software can proceed to a bounded patch. Hardware goes to workshop review. Missing evidence calls for more evidence.

32
00:03:43.649 --> 00:03:49.058
The classification is implemented; only the software branch is demonstrated by the completed live fixture.

33
00:03:49.738 --> 00:03:58.350
The Workshop planner allocates declared parts, technician time, and budget. Today it is browser-local, without automatic inventory synchronization into Review.

34
00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:03.454
A shared record connecting planning, diagnosis, and verified repair outcomes is future work.

35
00:04:04.134 --> 00:04:12.547
An outsider organization request was rejected. LangSmith traces keep metadata, excluding source and diagnostic narratives from trace inputs and outputs.

36
00:04:12.727 --> 00:04:18.175
These are specific controls and checks. An independent security assessment is still future work.

37
00:04:18.855 --> 00:04:24.383
Start with one owned diagnostic project, one repeatable failure, versioned evidence, and a separate reviewer.

38
00:04:24.563 --> 00:04:32.611
A commercial hypothesis is a supervised paid pilot, then a company subscription with explicit usage caps. Pricing and demand remain unvalidated.

39
00:04:33.291 --> 00:04:42.009
Google login works; custom company email remains deferred. Managed SSO needs identity-policy enforcement and deprovisioning, plus provider setup.

40
00:04:42.189 --> 00:04:49.047
Runner coverage remains constrained. Customer repair rates and field savings need real pilots measured against recorded baselines.

41
00:04:49.727 --> 00:05:01.609
Begin customer pilots alongside reliable onboarding. Next, fund recoverable jobs, cancellation, reconciled usage, and evaluations. Then enforced SSO, shared data, and broader tested runners.

42
00:05:01.789 --> 00:05:06.523
Dedicated Nebius capacity is a future option when measured load or residency needs justify it.

43
00:05:07.203 --> 00:05:16.425
Measure accepted fixes, regressions, review time, and total cost per accepted fix: inference, verification, and support, including rejected attempts.

44
00:05:16.605 --> 00:05:23.559
Each funded stage needs an acceptance gate. Measure physical throughput separately; environmental claims require field evidence.

45
00:05:24.239 --> 00:05:30.911
SpareCycle Review connects asset evidence to controlled software changes, using real Nebius inference and NVIDIA Nemotron reasoning.

46
00:05:31.091 --> 00:05:37.685
Inspect the product, draft, backend evidence, and roadmap. The next step is a focused pilot with measurable outcomes.
